            <ArticleTitle>Headache with autonomic features in a child: cluster headache or contact-point headache?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>473-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1526-4610.2007.01028.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Headache and facial pain due to diseases of the nose and sinuses are not uncommon in children. However, nasal contact-point associated with headache is relatively uncommon and has unclear etiological significance. We herein report a child having headache with autonomic features and contact-point in the nose, and discuss the difficulties in diagnostic categorization.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
